WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:04.400
- [Narrator] Ah, the
beautiful game of soccer.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.037
Or as the rest of the
world calls it, football.

00:00:07.924 --> 00:00:10.282
Football is a difficult game.

00:00:10.282 --> 00:00:11.970
And like any difficult game,

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.513
it has difficult moves.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:16.620
But the hardest move of them all is the

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.720
awe-inspiring, God-like,

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.963
unparalleled, bicycle kick.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:24.950
But the origins of this
world-class move are

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:28.730
sharply contested between
three Latin American countries.

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:29.940
So, we wanna know:

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.143
Who invented the bicycle kick?

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.180
Brazil, Peru, Chile.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.260
You see, depending on who you ask,

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.460
the bicycle kick has
three different names and

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:41.053
a contested origins story.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:43.750
If you go to Latin America,

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:46.060
many people will call
this kick La Chilena,

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:47.653
which means The Chilean.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.150
And it's all because of this guy:

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:51.940
Ramón Unzaga.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.593
The year is 1914.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.320
Popular tradition has it that

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.200
Unzaga invented the kick in
a football match in Chile.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.060
The Argentinean press took
notice of this spectacular play

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:06.110
and decided to adopt
the move as La Chilena,

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.545
which means The Chilean.

00:01:08.545 --> 00:01:10.128
- Goal! La Chilena!

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.230
- [Narrator] But, in Peru,

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:17.950
Peruvians will claim the
kick was invented in Callao,

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:19.270
Peru's largest port.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:21.033
It was the year 1892.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:23.654
Many believe that a Chalaco,

00:01:23.654 --> 00:01:25.320
as Callao locals are known,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.490
was the first to try
this acrobatic maneuver

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:30.300
in a game against British sailors.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.290
Some say that Chilean
spectators witnessed this move,

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:35.610
which Peruvians called, La Chalaca,

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:36.910
and took it back to Chile.

00:01:37.755 --> 00:01:41.310
(upbeat classical music)

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.020
Now, In Brazil,

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:44.983
they will give you their own story.

00:01:48.513 --> 00:01:50.640
(whistles blowing)

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.030
Some have suggested that the
Brazilian Leonidas da Silva

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:56.810
was the first to ever develop

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:58.923
what Brazilians call, Bicicleta.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:02.370
Word is, when Leonidas made the move,

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.650
the referee was so startled,

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.863
he didn't know if it
was even a legal play.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:11.960
The truth is that it's
really hard to pin down the

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:15.110
inception of this amazing move.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:16.410
And whether its origin's are

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.930
Chilean, Peruvian, or Brazilian,

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.470
one thing's for sure:

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:24.547
the bicycle kick is truly
a Latin American invention.

00:02:25.458 --> 00:02:28.125
(whistle blows)

